ELEMENTARY TRANSCENDENTAL FUNCTIONS (x^{2}\times 1)
4.6.7  arctanh  z=arcoth  z+1  zri (see 4.5.60)  (according as \mathcal{F}_{z\geq 0})
Fundamental Property
The general solutions of the equations
z=sinh t
z=cosh t
z=tanh t
are respectively
4.6.8   t=Arcsinh z=(-1)^{k}\arcsinh z+k\pi i
4.6.9   t=Arccosh z=\pm\arccosh z+2k\pi i
4.6.10   t=Artcahn z=\arctanh z+k\pi i
Functions of Negative Arguments
4.6.11  arcsinh (-z)=-arcsinh z
4.6.12  arccosh (-z)=arccosh z
4.6.13  arctanh (-z)=-arctanh z
Relation to Inverse Circular Functions (see 4.4.20 to 4.4.25)
Hyperbolic identities can be derived from trigonometric identities by replacing \(z\) by \(iz\).
4.6.14  Arcsinh z=-i Arcsin iz
4.6.15  Arccosh z=\pm i Arccos z
4.6.16  Arctanh z=-i Arctan iz
4.6.17  Arcesch z=i Arcesc iz
4.6.18  Arcesch z=\pm i Arcsec z
4.6.19  Arccoth z=i Arccot iz
Logarithmic Representations
4.6.20  arcsinh x=\ln [x+(x^{2}+1)^{k}]
4.6.21  arccosh x=\ln [x+(x^{k}-1)^{k}] (x\geq 1)
4.6.22  arctanh x=\frac12\ln\frac{1+x}{1-x} (0\leq x^{k}<1)
4.6.23  arccsch z=\ln [\frac{1}{x}+(\frac{1}{x^{2}}+1)^{\frac{k}{2}}] (x\neq 0)
4.6.24  arcsech z=\ln [\frac{1}{x}+(\frac{1}{x^{2}}-1)^{\frac{k}{2}}] (0<x\leq 1)
Addition and Subtraction of Two Inverse Hyperbolic Functions
4.6.26
Arcsinh z_{1}\pm Arcsinh z_{2}
=Arcsinh [z_{1}(1+z_{2}^{2})^{k}\pm z_{2}(1+z_{1}^{2})^{k}]
4.6.27
Arccosh z_{1}\pm Arccosh z_{2}
=Arccosh [z_{1}z_{2}\pm[(z_{1}^{2}-1)(z_{2}^{2}-1)^{k}]]
4.6.28
Arctanh z_{1}\pm Arctanh z_{2}=Arctanh (\frac{z_{1}\pm z_{2}}{1\pm z_{1}z_{2}})
4.6.29
Arcsinh z_{1}\pm Arccosh z_{2}
=Arcsinh [z_{1}z_{2}\pm[(1+z_{1}^{2})(z_{2}^{2}-1)^{k}]]
4.6.30
Arctanh z_{1}\pm Arccosh z_{2}=Arctanh (\frac{z_{1}z_{2}\pm 1}{z_{2}\pm z_{1}})
=Arccoth (\frac{z_{1}z_{2}\pm z_{1}}{z_{1}z_{2}\pm z_{1}})
